{"type":"tool_result","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-a587834b1fdeb189","status":"completed","result":"=== SKILL headings/refs ===\n131:It exits `0` on candidates from either source, `1` on a clean no-hit and `2` on a bad invocation or unreadable index.\n169:- Tests may mutate env vars, but must restore them in `afterEach`. Production code paths should not treat mutable process env as request-time configuration.\n200:1. **Determine level (1/2/3/3+) before ANY file changes** - Count LOC, assess complexity/risk\n216:    - Suggest `/speckit:plan :with-phases` only when phase complexity score >= 25 AND documentation level >= 3.\n287:| Create in a sub-track | Add `--track <track>/<sub>` for a declared sub-track. Numbering restarts inside it, and `suggest-subtracks.mjs` names the best-fitting sub-track for a description |\n288:| Track-root lists | `node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/refresh-track-roots.mjs --apply` after moving, renaming or deleting a packet in a track by hand; `archive.sh` refreshes it itself. The pre-push hook blocks a commit whose track lists other packets than it holds |\n292:| Gate 1 trigger lookup | `node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/retrieval/lookup-trigger-index.mjs --json -- \"<prompt>\"` (exit `0` hit, `1` no-hit, `2` broken) |\n"}
{"type":"tool_call","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-841b1e1ba4dce4bd","tool":"bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled; echo '=== README refs ==='; grep -n \"relocat\\|archive\\|subtrack\\|sub-track\\|include-archive\\|z_archive\\|renumber\\|restore\\|suggest-subtracks\\|exit\" README.md .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/README.md .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/README.md | head -300","description":"Search READMEs for affected behavior"}}
{"type":"tool_result","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-841b1e1ba4dce4bd","status":"completed","result":"=== README refs ===\nREADME.md:270:Documentation depth scales with task complexity.\nREADME.md:277:| **Level 3+** | complexity score 80+ | Level 3 file set plus governance sections inside `spec.md` | High-complexity work needing review tracking and workstream coordination |\nREADME.md:279:The LOC ranges are guidance, not hard rules. Risk, complexity and the number of affected files can push a task to a higher level. When in doubt, choose the higher level.\nREADME.md:371:`check-completion.sh` reads the verification section of `tasks.md` and requires every item to carry a P0, P1 or P2 tag. A Level 1 packet without that section exits the check unenforced.\nREADME.md:416:- **`relocate.mjs`** - move, renumber or re-parent spec packets and rewrite every store that records the path. Takes `--map <file.json>`, `--from` with `--to`, `--renumber <folder>` or `--declare-subtrack <track>/<name>`. Dry run by default, `--apply` writes\nREADME.md:417:- **`suggest-subtracks.mjs`** - propose a whole-track sub-track map for a flat track, or with `--describe` name the declared sub-track a new packet fits best. Writes nothing\nREADME.md:419:**`validate.sh` exit codes**\nREADME.md:430:`--verbose` shows details behind each rule. `--recursive` validates a parent and all child phase folders. `--include-archive` works only with `--recursive` and also validates the packets in the `z_archive/` of the folder given and, for a track root, of each declared sub-track. `validate.sh` implements no rules itself. It hands every rule decision to a single Node orchestrator.\nREADME.md:478:  │  C) Related, a phase child, a sub-track     │\nREADME.md:530:The trigger-index generator names the checkout it indexes. Run from a project that links the Skilled source, it indexes the source checkout unless `--repo-root` names another. It now prints one stderr line naming both roots and the `--repo-root` and `--out` flags. The default root, stdout and exit codes are unchanged.\nREADME.md:709:      │                               │  live / stale / absent / archived\nREADME.md:817:It keeps a vendored copy's own changes. `/doctor:overlay` reports an overlay that drifted and restores it, and it re-pins the copy to a newer upstream commit. It can also audit drift no overlay owns, move a source kept off its layout path, and take over a lock a run that has ended left behind. See [Copy It Into Your Project](INSTALL-SKILLED.md#4-copy-it-into-your-project). The other six doctor commands stay with the skill whose own checks they run, such as `/doctor:git` with `sk-git`. The [Doctor](#doctor) section lists them.\nREADME.md:1205:Two lexical lanes over spec docs and skill docs: trigger-index lookup with `--triggers`, which on a zero-hit also checks spec docs the index does not list yet, ripgrep recipes otherwise. `--paths` and `--count` pick the recipe, and `--packet <specFolder>` narrows the search roots. Archived packets are left out by default. `--include-archive` searches them as well through `rg-wrapper.mjs`, marks each archived path `[archived]` and lists the `description.json` and `graph-metadata.json` files of archived packets that hold the phrase. A phrase nobody wrote is a clean no-hit, never a nearest guess.\nREADME.md:1310:| `/doctor:agents-md` | `check`, `embed`, `restore` | `check` | `embed`, `restore` |\nREADME.md:1342:- `rebuild` rebuilds `skill-graph.sqlite` from the checked-in `graph-metadata.json` files through the advisor CLI, after a backup it restores when the rebuild fails\nREADME.md:1398:- `repin --rollback <run-id>` restores that backup after its own yes\nREADME.md:1402:**`/doctor:agents-md [check|embed|restore] [--json] [--root <dir>] [--dry-run] [--plan-digest <digest>] [--run <dir>] [--yes]`** - your root `AGENTS.md` against the shipped template\nREADME.md:1405:- `embed` composes in memory and shows every target with its diff, the new block, the byte budget and the lines it will keep. Then it prints a plan digest. It writes only when you give that digest back and backs up every path it touches first. Afterwards it proves that not one of your lines was dropped, and restores from the backup itself when one is\nREADME.md:1406:- `restore` restores exactly the paths one recorded run wrote, and removes the files, and the empty folders, that run created\nREADME.md:1624:- **Validation.** `SPECKIT_SKIP_VALIDATION=1` switches spec folder validation off and `SKDOC_SKIP_VALIDATION=1` switches off the sk-doc validators. A skipped run says so and exits 0, so it is no evidence that a document is valid. CI sets neither\nREADME.md:1746:- **[→ Daemon CLI Reference](.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/cli/daemon-cli-reference.md)** - the advisor's daemon-backed CLI front door, its warm-only policy and its exit codes\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/README.md:49:System Spec Kit captures every file-modifying conversation in a templated spec folder at one of four documentation levels matched to task complexity. Those decisions stay findable across sessions because they are committed files: a generated trigger index matches a prompt against author-declared phrases, and ripgrep finds anything else. `/speckit:resume` rebuilds the active context from packet-local sources. `/speckit:save` routes session updates into canonical documentation surfaces so the next session picks up where the last one stopped, on any model or tool.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/README.md:69:| **Documentation levels** | four levels matched to task complexity, from the Level 1 baseline up to the Level 3+ governance set |\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/README.md:162:The default validation set runs the non-strict rules from the 42-rule registry. Exit 0 means all rules pass, exit 1 is a user error, exit 2 is a validation error and exit 3 is a system error. `--quiet` prints the result line only. A `--strict` run on a Draft packet may first run `repair-derived.cjs --apply` to rewrite derived metadata, and `SPECKIT_DRAFT_AUTO_REPAIR=0` turns that off.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/README.md:168:  --json -- \"spec folder\"; echo \"exit=$?\"\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/README.md:243:The track folder itself, `specs/<track>/`, holds no spec docs. Its `graph-metadata.json` lists the track's packets in `children_ids`. `create.sh --track` adds each new packet to that list, `archive.sh` refreshes it after moving a packet into the track's own `z_archive/` or back, `refresh-track-roots.mjs --apply` rewrites it from the packet folders on disk, and the pre-push hook blocks a pushed commit whose list disagrees with the packets it holds.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/README.md:245:A track can also hold one declared sub-track level, `specs/<track>/<sub-track>/<NNN-name>/`. A sub-track is a non-numbered child with no `spec.md`, `description.json` at level `sub-track` and the same `graph-metadata.json` shape as a track root, and the track lists it in `children_ids`. It numbers its own packets from `001`, keeps its own `z_archive/`, and is skipped with one diagnostic when it is a symlink. Undeclared non-numbered folders are reported, never promoted. One level only.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/README.md:249:Not every change needs the same amount of paperwork. A one-line bug fix does not need an architecture decision record. A multi-system refactor does. Four levels match documentation depth to task complexity:\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/README.md:256:| **3+** | complexity 80 and up | Level 3 plus approval workflow, compliance and stakeholders | high-complexity work needing review tracking |\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/README.md:259:The LOC ranges are guidance, not hard rules. Risk, complexity and the number of affected files can push a task to a higher level. When in doubt, choose the higher level.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/README.md:332:- **Free-text retrieval:** the ripgrep recipes in `references/retrieval/retrieval-conventions.md` find a phrase anywhere in the corpus. The `rg-wrapper.mjs` structured recipe shows each file once at its best line, cuts at `--limit` files (default 20, `--limit 0` uncapped) and reports the hidden files and matches. The path-only and count recipes take the same limit. Archived packets are left o","truncated":true}
{"type":"tool_call","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-86b1730966a7b224","tool":"bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled; echo '=== refs ==='; grep -n \"relocat\\|archive\\|subtrack\\|sub-track\\|include-archive\\|z_archive\\|renumber\\|restore\\|suggest-subtracks\\|exit 3\\|exit status\" .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/phase-definitions.md .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/quick-reference.md .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/worked-examples.md .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/retrieval/retrieval-conventions.md .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/memory/trigger-config.md | head -400","description":"Search references for affected behavior"}}
{"type":"tool_result","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-86b1730966a7b224","status":"completed","result":"=== refs ===\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:99:A track may hold one optional level of named sub-tracks between itself and its packets:\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:104:├── <sub-track>/\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:105:│   ├── 001-<slug>/                # numbering restarts inside the sub-track\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:107:└── z_archive/\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:110:A sub-track is declared, not inferred. All of these hold:\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:115:| `description.json` | `\"level\": \"sub-track\"` and `specFolder` set to `<track>/<sub-track>`. `specId` is not required: the description producers write only `specFolder`, and no reader derives a sub-track identity from `specId`. |\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:116:| `graph-metadata.json` | Track-root shape: `packet_id` `<track>/<sub-track>`, `parent_id` the track, `children_ids` its immediate numbered packets |\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:117:| Track registration | The sub-track id appears in the track's `graph-metadata.json` `children_ids` |\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:119:Numbering continues per folder, so each sub-track numbers its own packets from `001`. One level is supported: a sub-track holds numbered packets, not another sub-track. A declared folder that contains `spec.md` gets the diagnostic `declared folder has spec.md and cannot be a sub-track`, and `create.sh` refuses it.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:121:A track's `children_ids` lists its direct numbered packets and its declared sub-tracks. A sub-track's `children_ids` lists only its own immediate numbered packets. A numbered phase parent counts as one entry, and its phases stay its own children.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:123:A sub-track has its own `z_archive/`. Any `z_` folder inside a track or sub-track is never a sub-track and is never discovered as content. A non-numbered folder without the declaration gets one diagnostic and is never promoted. A symlinked sub-track is skipped with one diagnostic in both the working-tree and the commit view. The sweep treats these diagnostics as advisory and does not change its exit code. `create.sh` uses sub-track diagnostics to explain invalid `--track` values and exits with status 1.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:127:A sub-track is a proposal the operator approves, never a move made silently. Propose one at two moments.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:129:**When a packet is created.** `create.sh` prints one advisory line to stderr when the track has grown into clusters, when the new packet fits no existing sub-track, or when it fits a different sub-track better than the one named. The line never blocks the scaffold. Ask the same question directly with:\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:132:node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/suggest-subtracks.mjs --track <track> --describe \"<what the packet is for>\"\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:138:node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/suggest-subtracks.mjs --track <track> > map.json\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:141:For a flat track, the map names every live packet of the track exactly once, because a track holds either packets or sub-tracks and never both. The tool prints no map in three cases, each with one line and exit `0`: a track below the threshold, a track where no grouping is found, and a track that already has a declared sub-track.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:143:A track that already has a declared sub-track gets `track <track> is already grouped into sub-tracks; no map`, also when numbered packets still sit beside the sub-tracks. For such a track, ask where each packet fits with `--describe`, which prints `best fit: <track>/<sub-track>` or `none fits: propose a new sub-track in <track>`. Then write the map by hand, one `{ \"from\": \"<track>/NNN-slug\", \"to\": \"<track>/<sub-track>/NNN-slug\" }` entry for every packet still at the track level, and run it through `relocate.mjs --map`. The map must cover all of them, because `relocate.mjs` refuses a plan that leaves flat packets beside sub-tracks. A single remaining packet can move with `relocate.mjs --from <path> --to <path>`.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:145:Rename the candidate sub-track names when a clearer name exists, show the map to the operator, and apply the approved map with the relocation tool:\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:148:node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/relocate.mjs --map map.json          # dry run, writes nothing\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:149:node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/relocate.mjs --map map.json --apply\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:152:`relocate.mjs` declares each new sub-track, moves and renumbers the packets and rewrites every store that records a packet path. Do not move a packet by hand.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:306:Completed or superseded specs use the `z_archive/` prefix:\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:312:└── z_archive/\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:317:A declared sub-track archives into its own `z_archive/`, not the track's:\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:323:│   └── z_archive/\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:325:└── z_archive/\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:418:├── invoicing/                       # declared sub-track\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:419:│   ├── description.json             # level: \"sub-track\"\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:425:└── z_archive/\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/phase-definitions.md:72:A series parent groups changes to one artifact. Grouping a whole track's packets by theme is a sub-track instead, and [folder-structure.md](folder-structure.md) says when to propose one under \"When to Propose a Sub-Track\".\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/quick-reference.md:139:# In a declared sub-track, numbered from 001 inside it:\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/quick-reference.md:231:| `archived` | Historical record | 5 (do not reuse) |\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/quick-reference.md:267:**Sub-Tracks:** If the work belongs to a track that is grouped into sub-tracks, prefer Option C and place the packet in the sub-track that fits. If the track's packets have grown into clusters and no sub-track fits, prefer Option C and propose a new sub-track as a whole-track map (`folder-structure.md` §2, When to Propose a Sub-Track). A sub-track is a proposal the operator approves, never a move made silently.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/quick-reference.md:279:- **Option C:** Use another existing packet — a related spec when it is a better scope match, a specific child under an existing phase parent, a related standard packet decomposed into phases when it meets both phase thresholds, a sub-track of the track (an existing one, or a new one proposed as a whole-track map when the track's packets have grown into clusters), or a series parent when the work is a different change to ","truncated":true}
