{"type":"tool_result","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-b2f98ca954abc99c","status":"completed","result":"=== feature relocation ===\n---\ntitle: \"Spec packet relocation\"\ndescription: \"Moves, renumbers and re-parents spec packets and rewrites every store that records the packet path, as a dry run unless --apply is passed.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"Spec packet relocation\"\n  - \"relocate.mjs\"\n  - \"move or renumber a spec packet\"\n  - \"declare a sub-track\"\n  - \"re-parent packets into a sub-track\"\nversion: 1.0.0.0\n---\n\n# Spec packet relocation (relocate.mjs)\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\nMoves, renumbers and re-parents spec packets and rewrites every store that records the packet path, as a dry run unless --apply is passed.\n\nA packet's path is written in many places, so a folder moved by hand leaves most of them naming the old place. `relocate.mjs` owns the order of operations and drives the writer that already owns each store. It is the tool an operator runs to regroup a track into sub-tracks, and the tool `archive.sh` calls when a restored packet needs a new number.\n\n---\n\n## 2. HOW IT WORKS\n\n### Modes\n\nThe tool runs from the repository root and takes exactly one mode. Paths are relative to the specs root, and a leading `specs/` is accepted.\n\n| Mode | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `--map <file.json>` | Applies a list of `{ \"from\", \"to\" }` moves. A destination in a sub-track that does not exist yet declares that sub-track in the same run |\n| `--from <path> --to <path>` | Moves one packet. Both flags are needed together |\n| `--renumber <folder>` | Closes the number gaps among a folder's numbered children in their current order. Numbers held in that folder's `z_archive/` count as taken |\n| `--declare-subtrack <track>/<name>` | Declares a sub-track in a track that holds no flat packets |\n\n`--apply` writes. Without it the run prints every move and every file it would change and writes nothing. `--help` prints the usage line and exits 0.\n\n### What a move rewrites\n\nAn applied plan runs in a fixed order, and each step prints `ok` or `FAILED`:\n\n1. New sub-tracks are declared: a `description.json` with level `sub-track` and a `z_archive/.gitkeep`.\n2. Each folder moves, through `git mv` when git tracks it and a plain rename when it does not.\n3. References to the old path are rewritten in the text files under `specs/`, and a phase parent's `children_ids` drops or adds the moved child. An old id is matched as a whole path, so a longer id that starts with it is left alone.\n4. The moved packets' own recorded paths are re-derived: `description.json` through the generated-JSON migration, then graph ids and document packet pointers through `repair-derived.cjs`. Packets whose references changed are re-derived too.\n5. The affected track and sub-track roots are refreshed through `refresh-track-roots.mjs`.\n6. The moves are recorded in the relocation ledger, the citation redirect table is rebuilt and the trigger index is regenerated.\n\nThe run then closes with `sweep-track-roots.mjs` and `validate.sh --strict` on each moved packet, and prints each packet's result line last.\n\nCode and CI files are never rewritten. A moved path named in the compiled route layout or in a workflow file under `.github/workflows` refuses the run, and the message names the file and line to update by hand.\n\n### Refusals and exit codes\n\nEvery refusal happens before the first write, each is printed as `relocate: refused: ...` and the run ends with `nothing was written`. The tool refuses:\n\n- a path outside `specs/` or one that passes through a symbolic link\n- a source or destination name that is not `NNN-slug`\n- a destination that already exists, or a number a live or archived sibling already holds\n- a source or destination named by two entries, a chain or swap between entries, or a destination inside its own source\n- a destination deeper than one sub-track, or in a folder that exists without the sub-track declaration\n- a plan that would leave a track holding flat packets beside sub-tracks\n- a tracked source with uncommitted changes, or an uncommitted file the run would rewrite\n- `--declare-subtrack` on a track without `graph-metadata.json`, on a folder that holds `spec.md` or on a track that still holds flat packets\n\nExit status 0 means nothing is left to do, and after `--apply` it means every step landed and the close passed. Status 1 means a dry run found work. Status 2 means a rejected argument, a refused plan, a failed step or a failed close.\n\nThe run is safe to repeat. An entry whose folder already sits at its destination is checked for stale records only, a second `--apply` of an applied map finds nothing to do, and a run that stopped on a failed step is finished by running the same map again.\n\n---\n\n## 3. SOURCE FILES\n\n### Implementation\n\n| File | Layer | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/relocate.mjs` | Script | Parses the mode, checks the plan, moves folders, rewrites references and drives the store writers |\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/lib/track-roots.mjs` | Shared | Reads tracks and sub-tracks and decides whether a track layout is mixed |\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/repair-derived.cjs` | Script | Re-derives graph ids and packet pointers for moved packets and packets whose references changed |\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/graph/migrate-generated-json.ts` | Script | Rewrites the path identity in each moved packet's `description.json` |\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/refresh-track-roots.mjs` | Script | Refreshes the track and sub-track children lists the moves change |\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/retrieval/generate-trigger-index.mjs` | Script | Regenerates the trigger index after the moves |\n| `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/scripts/cite-drift-scan.mjs` | Script | Rebuilds the citation redirect table from the relocation ledger |\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/archive.sh` | Script | Calls the tool to restore an archived packet onto the next free number |\n\n### Validation And Tests\n\n| File | Type | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/relocate.vitest.ts` | Vitest | Covers move, renumber, re-parent, sub-track declaration, the refusals, the dry-run exit status and a repeated apply |\n\n---\n\n## 4. SOURCE METADATA\n\n- Group: Tooling And Scripts\n- Canonical catalog source: `feature-catalog.md`\n- Feature file path: `tooling-and-scripts/spec-packet-relocation.md`\n\nRelated references:\n- [sub-track-proposal.md](sub-track-proposal.md) - Prints the map this tool applies.\n- [spec-lifecycle-automation.md](spec-lifecycle-automation.md) - Covers the archive restore that calls this tool.\n- [track-root-children-lists.md](track-root-children-lists.md) - Covers the children lists this tool refreshes.\n=== playbook relocation ===\n---\ntitle: \"492 -- Spec packet relocation and renumbering\"\ndescription: \"This scenario validates spec packet relocation for `492`. It focuses on the relocate.mjs dry run, a refused move onto an archived number and an applied renumber, all inside a throwaway copy of the checkout.\"\nversion: 1.0.0.1\n---\n\n# 492 -- Spec packet relocation and renumbering\n\nThis document captures the throwaway-copy manual validation contract for `relocate.mjs`.\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\nThis scenario validates spec packet relocation for `492`. It focuses on the `relocate.mjs` dry run, a refused move onto an archived number and an applied renumber. Every command runs inside a throwaway copy of the checkout under the operating-system temp directory. It never moves or rewrites anything in this checkout's `specs/` tree.\n\n### Why This Matters\n\nA packet's path is recorded in its own `description.json` and `graph-metadata.json`, in other packets' references, in the track root and in the relocation ledger. A folder moved by hand leaves most of those naming the old place. The tool is dry by default and refuses before the first write, so an operator can read the whole plan before anything changes.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SCENARIO CONTRACT\n\nOperators run the exact prompt and command sequence for `492` and confirm each outcome without contradictory evidence.\n\n- Objective: Prove that `relocate.mjs` prints a complete plan and writes nothing without `--apply`, refuses a d","truncated":true}
{"type":"tool_call","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-92ef4c6001d004ee","tool":"bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled; echo '=== feature archive search ==='; cat .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/feature-catalog/retrieval/rg-archive-search.md; echo '=== playbook archive search ==='; cat .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/manual-testing-playbook/retrieval/rg-archive-search.md; echo '=== feature proposal ==='; cat .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/feature-catalog/tooling-and-scripts/sub-track-proposal.md; echo '=== playbook proposal ==='; cat .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/manual-testing-playbook/tooling-and-scripts/sub-track-proposal.md","description":"Read archive search and proposal docs"}}
{"type":"tool_result","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-92ef4c6001d004ee","status":"completed","result":"=== feature archive search ===\n---\ntitle: \"Ripgrep archive search\"\ndescription: \"Searches archived packets on request, labels each archived hit and lists the archived packet metadata files that carry the phrase.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"Ripgrep archive search\"\n  - \"rg-wrapper --include-archive\"\n  - \"search archived spec packets\"\n  - \"archived label in ripgrep results\"\nversion: 1.0.0.0\n---\n\n# Ripgrep archive search (rg-wrapper.mjs --include-archive)\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\nSearches archived packets on request, labels each archived hit and lists the archived packet metadata files that carry the phrase.\n\nEvery recipe leaves `z_archive/` folders out by default, so an archived packet is invisible to a normal search. The `--include-archive` flag is the opt-in for a caller who needs to know whether finished work already covered a phrase.\n\n---\n\n## 2. HOW IT WORKS\n\n### Archived hits\n\n`--include-archive` works with the structured, path-only and count recipes. It drops only the archive exclusion from the recipe, so the other guards and the file limit apply as before. A path counts as archived when one of its segments is exactly `z_archive`.\n\nIn human output each archived path ends with `[archived]`. In JSON the report carries `includeArchive: true`, and each row in `results` and in `counts` carries `archived: true` or `archived: false`. The `paths` list of the path-only recipe stays a list of plain strings. Without the flag none of these fields appear, and the report shape is unchanged.\n\n### Metadata lane\n\nThe flag also runs a second search for the same phrase over the `description.json` and `graph-metadata.json` files of archived packets. It is a separate ripgrep run, because one run cannot combine two output modes, and a caller cannot name it as a recipe.\n\nHuman output lists its matches under a `json    :` line that gives the number of archived metadata files, with one path per line and each marked `[archived]`. JSON carries them as `jsonLane`, with the command, exit code, outcome, sorted paths and stderr of that run. The lane is not bounded by `--limit`, so every matching file is listed.\n\n### Edge cases\n\nWhen the main recipe finds nothing in markdown and the metadata lane finds a match, the run reports a match and the main recipe's own fields stay shaped as a miss. When either run fails, the run reports an error. If both fail, the main recipe's exit status and stderr are the ones reported.\n\n---\n\n## 3. SOURCE FILES\n\n### Implementation\n\n| File | Layer | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/retrieval/rg-wrapper.mjs` | Script | Parses the flag, builds the archive-inclusive recipes and the metadata lane, labels archived paths and formats both outputs |\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/retrieval/lib/rg-lane.mjs` | Shared | Ranks matches and keeps the first ranked row for each path, before archived rows are labelled |\n\n### Validation And Tests\n\n| File | Type | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/rg-include-archive.vitest.ts` | Vitest | Covers the default exclusion, the archive-inclusive recipes, archive search behavior and flag parsing |\n\n---\n\n## 4. SOURCE METADATA\n\n- Group: Retrieval\n- Canonical catalog source: `feature-catalog.md`\n- Feature file path: `retrieval/rg-archive-search.md`\n\nRelated references:\n- [rg-flood-control.md](rg-flood-control.md) - Covers the per-file rows and file limit this mode keeps.\n- [stale-index-fallback.md](stale-index-fallback.md) - Covers the adjacent ripgrep retrieval experience.\n=== playbook archive search ===\n---\ntitle: \"494 -- Ripgrep archive search\"\ndescription: \"This scenario validates archive-aware search for `494`. It focuses on rg-wrapper.mjs leaving archived packets out by default and, with --include-archive, marking each archived hit and listing matching archived metadata files.\"\nversion: 1.0.0.0\n---\n\n# 494 -- Ripgrep archive search\n\nThis document captures the scratch-directory manual validation contract for `rg-wrapper.mjs --include-archive`.\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\nThis scenario validates archive-aware search for `494`. It focuses on `rg-wrapper.mjs` leaving archived packets out by default and, with `--include-archive`, marking each archived hit and listing matching archived metadata files. The fixture lives in a scratch directory under the operating-system temp directory, and the wrapper searches it through `--root`. It never writes to this checkout.\n\n### Why This Matters\n\nAn archived packet is finished or abandoned work. Returned unmarked beside live packets it reads as current evidence. Left out with no way back in, it hides the history of a decision. The default search keeps the archive out, and the opt-in search says which hits are archived.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SCENARIO CONTRACT\n\nOperators run the exact prompt and command sequence for `494` and confirm each outcome without contradictory evidence.\n\n- Objective: Prove that the default recipes skip `z_archive/`, and that `--include-archive` adds the archived hit with an `[archived]` mark, an `archived` field on each JSON row and a `json    :` section naming the archived packet's matching `description.json`.\n- Real user request: `Search the specs for ledger reconciliation, and this time include archived packets. Tell me which hits are archived.`\n- Prompt: `Search the demo specs for \"ledger reconciliation\" with rg-wrapper.mjs, first without and then with --include-archive. Tell me which hits are archived. Return PASS or FAIL with the paths and the exit codes.`\n- Expected execution process: Build one live packet and one archived packet that both carry the phrase in a scratch directory, run the path recipe without the flag, then run the path, structured and count recipes with it.\n- Expected signals: Without the flag the path recipe reports `paths   : 1` and its command carries `--glob '!**/z_archive/**'`. With the flag it reports `paths   : 2`, the archived path ends in `[archived]`, the command no longer carries that glob, and a `json    : 1 archived metadata file(s)` section follows. The count recipe's JSON rows carry `\"archived\": false` for the live file and `\"archived\": true` for the archived one.\n- Desired user-visible outcome: A concise `PASS` that names the live hit and the archived hit apart, or a `FAIL` that names the first violated check.\n- Pass/fail: PASS if every condition in Section 3 Pass / Fail holds, FAIL if any one does not.\n\n---\n\n## 3. TEST EXECUTION\n\n### Prompt\n\n```text\nSearch the demo specs for \"ledger reconciliation\" with rg-wrapper.mjs, first without and then with --include-archive. Tell me which hits are archived. Return PASS or FAIL with the paths and the exit codes.\n```\n\n### Commands\n\n1. From the repository root, create an isolated scratch directory. Confirm the printed path begins with the operating-system temp directory and is not the repository root.\n\n   ```bash\n   export RG_ARCHIVE_DEMO=\"$(cd \"$(mktemp -d \"${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/rg-archive-manual.XXXXXX\")\" && pwd -P)\"\n   printf 'RG_ARCHIVE_DEMO=%s\\n' \"$RG_ARCHIVE_DEMO\"\n   ```\n\n2. Build one live packet and one archived packet. Both carry the phrase in `spec.md`, and the archived packet also carries it in `description.json`.\n\n   ```bash\n   mkdir -p \"$RG_ARCHIVE_DEMO/specs/demo/001-alpha\" \"$RG_ARCHIVE_DEMO/specs/demo/z_archive/002-old\"\n   printf '# Alpha\\n\\nLedger reconciliation notes. Follow-up work lives in specs/demo/004-beta.\\n' > \"$RG_ARCHIVE_DEMO/specs/demo/001-alpha/spec.md\"\n   printf '# Old\\n\\nLedger reconciliation, first attempt.\\n' > \"$RG_ARCHIVE_DEMO/specs/demo/z_archive/002-old/spec.md\"\n   printf '{\"level\":\"task\",\"specFolder\":\"demo/z_archive/002-old\",\"description\":\"Ledger reconciliation, first attempt.\"}\\n' > \"$RG_ARCHIVE_DEMO/specs/demo/z_archive/002-old/description.json\"\n   ```\n\n3. Run the path recipe without the flag.\n\n   ```bash\n   node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/retrieval/rg-wrapper.mjs path \"ledger reconciliation\" --root \"$RG_ARCHIVE_DEMO\" --search-root specs\n   echo \"DEFAULT_EXIT=$?\"\n   ```\n\n4. Run the path recipe with `--include-archive`.\n\n   ```bash\n   node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/retrieval/rg-wrapper.mjs path \"ledger reconciliation\" --root \"$RG_ARCHIVE_DEMO\" --search-roo","truncated":true}
