{"type":"tool_call","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-8bd469be5d7ca50b","tool":"bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled; for f in .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/SKILL.md .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/phase-definitions.md .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/quick-reference.md .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/worked-examples.md .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/retrieval/retrieval-conventions.md .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/README.md README.md .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/README.md; do echo \"===== $f =====\"; grep -n \"exit status 3\\|status 3\\|exit 3\\|archivedPaths\\|mixed-track\\|mixed track\\|pre-archive\\|bare name\\|bare-name\\|case-fold\\|case folded\\|case-insensitive duplicate\\|resume\\|late step\\|nothing to do\\|not NNN-slug\" \"$f\" || true; done","description":"Find specific new behavior terms in docs"}}
{"type":"tool_result","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-8bd469be5d7ca50b","status":"completed","result":"===== .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/SKILL.md =====\n40:- Positive keywords include add, implement, fix, update, create, modify, rename, delete and configure. The authoritative Gate 3 classifier intentionally omits `analyze`, `decompose` and `phase` from positive triggers; `analyze` is a read-only disqualifier unless a real write, memory-save or resume trigger is also present.\n92:A request that writes any file selects it, and so does a create, a save, a resume or a validation question.\n143:`/speckit:resume` owns the ladder. A compaction may add one bounded `## History Lookup` section when a fresh locator verifies, and that lookup never replaces the ladder.\n175:Startup, resume, hook, goal plugin, code graph and Code Graph readiness details live in `references/config/hook-system.md`, `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md`, `.skilled/hooks/goal/goal-plugin.md`, `runtime/hooks/README.md` and code graph references.\n===== .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md =====\n261:**Why:** the owning phase keeps its own deep-loop artifacts local, nested runs do not spill into ancestor roots, and resume/restart logic can stay bound to the exact target spec instead of re-resolving through a coordination parent.\n===== .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/phase-definitions.md =====\n===== .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/quick-reference.md =====\n115:- `/speckit:resume` - continue or recover existing spec-folder work\n229:| `paused` | Temporarily on hold | 3 (can resume) |\n499:**Phase-Parent Resume Ladder**: When the resume target is a phase parent (folder has `[0-9]{3}-name/` children with `spec.md` or `description.json`), `/speckit:resume` honors the pointer first:\n522:**Purpose:** Refresh packet continuity before a pause or handoff while keeping `handover.md`, `_memory.continuity`, and the packet docs aligned for the next `/speckit:resume` pass.\n===== .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/worked-examples.md =====\n246:5. Implementation/resume happens in the active child - root is coordination-only\n264:User: /speckit:resume specs/009-notification-system/\n318:| `/speckit:resume` | Continue from previous session |\n===== .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/retrieval/retrieval-conventions.md =====\n316:| `z_archive` | Yes | Yes (default) | Shared by default, and the default is unchanged. The trigger index always prunes it and resume excludes it. The ripgrep wrapper searches it only on request, with `--include-archive` (Section 2) |\n===== .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/README.md =====\n3:description: \"Makes AI development continuity durable: every file change gets a templated spec folder that records the reasoning. Every new session resumes that reasoning from the packet's own committed documents.\"\n29:| **Invoke with** | \"spec kit\", \"spec folder\", \"memory save\", \"/speckit:resume\" or automatic Gate 3 routing |\n49:System Spec Kit captures every file-modifying conversation in a templated spec folder at one of four documentation levels matched to task complexity. Those decisions stay findable across sessions because they are committed files: a generated trigger index matches a prompt against author-declared phrases, and ripgrep finds anything else. `/speckit:resume` rebuilds the active context from packet-local sources. `/speckit:save` routes session updates into canonical documentation surfaces so the next session picks up where the last one stopped, on any model or tool.\n90:- `skilled-loop` (`.skilled/bin/`) then relaunches a near-limit session in a fresh runtime in the same terminal, on its own for Claude Code, Codex, Pi and OpenCode and through `/speckit:handoff` for Cursor and Devin: the handoff note links to the bound packet, `/speckit:save` runs before the hop and `/speckit:resume` after the claim, while the seed carries the session's Gate 3 answer.\n142:/speckit:resume\n162:The default validation set runs the non-strict rules from the 42-rule registry. Exit 0 means all rules pass, exit 1 is a user error, exit 2 is a validation error and exit 3 is a system error. `--quiet` prints the result line only. A `--strict` run on a Draft packet may first run `repair-derived.cjs --apply` to rewrite derived metadata, and `SPECKIT_DRAFT_AUTO_REPAIR=0` turns that off.\n190:| Recover after crash | `/speckit:resume` | session interrupted unexpectedly |\n221:  └─► /speckit:resume compares handover.md and _memory.continuity freshness, then falls back to packet docs\n223:       └─► AI resumes with context + health + structural readiness\n237:├── handover.md                  # Operator-facing session handoff for /speckit:resume\n241:`implementation-summary.md` is required at all levels but created after implementation completes, not at folder creation time. `generate-context.js` updates the packet continuity state for `/speckit:resume`, refreshes `description.json.lastUpdated` and rewrites the derived fields in `graph-metadata.json` on every run. It is the only writer of those files, so metadata cannot lag behind a save that went through it.\n285:`/speckit:resume` reads the pointer first when the target is a phase parent and follows valid bare child ids or track-relative child paths under the parent. The redirect is bounded and escape-safe. Missing, malformed, stale-to-missing-child or non-child pointers leave resume on the requested folder instead of escaping the packet tree. Detection is a single source of truth: `is_phase_parent()` in shell and `isPhaseParent()` in ESM JavaScript MUST agree.\n333:- **Continuity:** `runtime/cli/dist/continuity/generate-context.js` writes it into the packet itself, and `/speckit:resume` reads it back through the ladder it walks.\n348:| `/speckit:resume` | 4 | resume a previous session on an existing spec folder |\n553:| Session context lost after crash | use `/speckit:resume` to select the fresher folder-local source |\n588:A: Spec folders capture what happened in structured documentation. `generate-context.js` updates the packet's canonical continuity surfaces. `/speckit:resume` rebuilds the next session from those sources, comparing folder-local `handover.md` and `_memory.continuity` freshness before falling back to packet docs. Deeper retrieval reads the same files directly: the trigger index for a declared phrase, the ripgrep recipes for anything else. One side captures, the recovery surfaces retrieve, and both work with nothing running.\n676:| [`references/workflows/intake-contract.md`](./references/workflows/intake-contract.md) | shared spec-folder intake contract for plan, complete and resume re-entry |\n===== README.md =====\n45:`/speckit:resume` reads the packet's own continuity files and picks up where the last session stopped. A committed trigger index plus ripgrep recipes find prior work fast, with no database and no daemon on this path. A phrase nobody wrote is a clean no-hit, never a nearest guess.\n206:This creates a spec folder, plans the work, builds it and saves the session context into the folder as it goes. Add `:with-research` to research before planning. In a later session, `/speckit:resume` picks up where the last one stopped.\n522:Canonical continuity lives in the spec packet itself. `generate-context.js` updates it and may emit supporting generated context artifacts inside the spec folder. `/speckit:resume` is the recovery surface. It rebuilds in this order: `handover.md` -> `_memory.continuity` -> canonical spec docs.\n548:`/speckit:resume` stays the primary recovery path. The history lookup only adds to it. When its handle is missing or stale, the section is left out and recovery runs as before.\n604:Progress is saved outside the chat, so you can pause and resume anytime. A loop survives crashes, new sessions and long runs. A loop ends only when the work has converged and passed its quality checks, never because an agent says it is done.\n606:Run fully autonomous with `:auto` or pause at each step with `:confirm`. You can start fresh, resume or restart at will.\n622:Runs are replayable. Convergence scores and verdicts recompute from the stored events, so a run can be audited, resumed or rebuilt after a cr","truncated":true}
{"type":"tool_call","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-ae6c4905023abcef","tool":"bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled; echo '=== folder structure section ==='; nl -ba .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md | sed -n '90,165p'; echo '=== folder archive section ==='; nl -ba .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md | sed -n '300,345p'; echo '=== retrieval conventions 70-90 ==='; nl -ba .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/retrieval/retrieval-conventions.md | sed -n '72,92p'","description":"Read folder structure and retrieval doc sections"}}
{"type":"tool_result","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-ae6c4905023abcef","status":"completed","result":"=== folder structure section ===\n    90\t```\n    91\t007-feature/\n    92\t├── 001-initial-implementation/\n    93\t├── 002-bug-fixes/\n    94\t└── 003-performance-optimization/\n    95\t```\n    96\t\n    97\t### Sub-Track Folders\n    98\t\n    99\tA track may hold one optional level of named sub-tracks between itself and its packets:\n   100\t\n   101\t```\n   102\tspecs/<track>/\n   103\t├── <NNN>-<slug>/                  # packet filed directly in the track\n   104\t├── <sub-track>/\n   105\t│   ├── 001-<slug>/                # numbering restarts inside the sub-track\n   106\t│   └── 002-<slug>/\n   107\t└── z_archive/\n   108\t```\n   109\t\n   110\tA sub-track is declared, not inferred. All of these hold:\n   111\t\n   112\t| Part | Requirement |\n   113\t|---|---|\n   114\t| Shape | Direct child of a track root, name not `NNN-*`, no `spec.md` |\n   115\t| `description.json` | `\"level\": \"sub-track\"` and `specFolder` set to `<track>/<sub-track>`. `specId` is not required: the description producers write only `specFolder`, and no reader derives a sub-track identity from `specId`. |\n   116\t| `graph-metadata.json` | Track-root shape: `packet_id` `<track>/<sub-track>`, `parent_id` the track, `children_ids` its immediate numbered packets |\n   117\t| Track registration | The sub-track id appears in the track's `graph-metadata.json` `children_ids` |\n   118\t\n   119\tNumbering continues per folder, so each sub-track numbers its own packets from `001`. One level is supported: a sub-track holds numbered packets, not another sub-track. A declared folder that contains `spec.md` gets the diagnostic `declared folder has spec.md and cannot be a sub-track`, and `create.sh` refuses it.\n   120\t\n   121\tA track's `children_ids` lists its direct numbered packets and its declared sub-tracks. A sub-track's `children_ids` lists only its own immediate numbered packets. A numbered phase parent counts as one entry, and its phases stay its own children.\n   122\t\n   123\tA sub-track has its own `z_archive/`. Any `z_` folder inside a track or sub-track is never a sub-track and is never discovered as content. A non-numbered folder without the declaration gets one diagnostic and is never promoted. A symlinked sub-track is skipped with one diagnostic in both the working-tree and the commit view. The sweep treats these diagnostics as advisory and does not change its exit code. `create.sh` uses sub-track diagnostics to explain invalid `--track` values and exits with status 1.\n   124\t\n   125\t### When to Propose a Sub-Track\n   126\t\n   127\tA sub-track is a proposal the operator approves, never a move made silently. Propose one at two moments.\n   128\t\n   129\t**When a packet is created.** `create.sh` prints one advisory line to stderr when the track has grown into clusters, when the new packet fits no existing sub-track, or when it fits a different sub-track better than the one named. The line never blocks the scaffold. Ask the same question directly with:\n   130\t\n   131\t```bash\n   132\tnode .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/suggest-subtracks.mjs --track <track> --describe \"<what the packet is for>\"\n   133\t```\n   134\t\n   135\t**When a flat track has grown.** A flat track with 8 or more live packets is a candidate. `--threshold <n>` changes that number. The tool clusters the track's packets by the terms their `description.json` and `graph-metadata.json` share and prints a whole-track map:\n   136\t\n   137\t```bash\n   138\tnode .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/suggest-subtracks.mjs --track <track> > map.json\n   139\t```\n   140\t\n   141\tFor a flat track, the map names every live packet of the track exactly once, because a track holds either packets or sub-tracks and never both. The tool prints no map in three cases, each with one line and exit `0`: a track below the threshold, a track where no grouping is found, and a track that already has a declared sub-track.\n   142\t\n   143\tA track that already has a declared sub-track gets `track <track> is already grouped into sub-tracks; no map`, also when numbered packets still sit beside the sub-tracks. For such a track, ask where each packet fits with `--describe`, which prints `best fit: <track>/<sub-track>` or `none fits: propose a new sub-track in <track>`. Then write the map by hand, one `{ \"from\": \"<track>/NNN-slug\", \"to\": \"<track>/<sub-track>/NNN-slug\" }` entry for every packet still at the track level, and run it through `relocate.mjs --map`. The map must cover all of them, because `relocate.mjs` refuses a plan that leaves flat packets beside sub-tracks. A single remaining packet can move with `relocate.mjs --from <path> --to <path>`.\n   144\t\n   145\tRename the candidate sub-track names when a clearer name exists, show the map to the operator, and apply the approved map with the relocation tool:\n   146\t\n   147\t```bash\n   148\tnode .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/relocate.mjs --map map.json          # dry run, writes nothing\n   149\tnode .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/relocate.mjs --map map.json --apply\n   150\t```\n   151\t\n   152\t`relocate.mjs` declares each new sub-track, moves and renumbers the packets and rewrites every store that records a packet path. Do not move a packet by hand.\n   153\t\n   154\t---\n   155\t\n   156\t## 3. LEVEL REQUIREMENTS\n   157\t\n   158\t### Level 1 (< 100 LOC)\n   159\t\n   160\t**Required Files:**\n   161\t```\n   162\tspecs/NNN-name/\n   163\t├── spec.md                    # Problem statement, goals, scope\n   164\t├── plan.md                    # Implementation approach\n   165\t├── tasks.md                   # Task breakdown\n=== folder archive section ===\n   300\t**See also:** `system-deep-loop/deep-research/references/protocol/loop-protocol.md`, `system-deep-loop/deep-review/references/protocol/loop-protocol.md`, and the `step_resolve_artifact_root` block in `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-research-auto.yaml`.\n   301\t\n   302\t---\n   303\t\n   304\t## 5. ARCHIVE PATTERN\n   305\t\n   306\tCompleted or superseded specs use the `z_archive/` prefix:\n   307\t\n   308\t```\n   309\tspecs/\n   310\t├── 001-active-feature/\n   311\t├── 002-in-progress/\n   312\t└── z_archive/\n   313\t    ├── 001-completed-feature/\n   314\t    └── 002-abandoned-approach/\n   315\t```\n   316\t\n   317\tA declared sub-track archives into its own `z_archive/`, not the track's:\n   318\t\n   319\t```\n   320\tspecs/demo/\n   321\t├── sub/\n   322\t│   ├── 001-active/\n   323\t│   └── z_archive/\n   324\t│       └── 001-retired/\n   325\t└── z_archive/\n   326\t    └── 001-old-track-packet/\n   327\t```\n   328\t\n   329\t**Archive Triggers:**\n   330\t- Feature fully implemented and verified\n   331\t- Approach abandoned for alternative\n   332\t- Spec superseded by newer version\n   333\t\n   334\t---\n   335\t\n   336\t## 6. EXAMPLE STRUCTURES\n   337\t\n   338\t### Simple Feature (Level 1)\n   339\t\n   340\t```\n   341\tspecs/015-add-dark-mode/\n   342\t├── spec.md\n   343\t├── plan.md\n   344\t├── tasks.md\n   345\t└── implementation-summary.md  # Created after implementation\n=== retrieval conventions 70-90 ===\n    72\t\n    73\tNothing in this document may be read as a claim that the two index lanes plus grep cover the rows above.\n    74\t\n    75\t---\n    76\t\n    77\t## 2. THE RECIPES\n    78\t\n    79\tEvery recipe below is literal. Copy the flags rather than paraphrasing them, because `--no-config`, the exclusion globs and the `--` separator each close a specific failure.\n    80\t\n    81\t- `--no-config` is mandatory. Without it `RIPGREP_CONFIG_PATH` can inject arguments the caller never wrote.\n    82\t- `--hidden` is mandatory. The `.skilled` root holds dotted directories with live documentation, and without the flag ripgrep skips them without a word, so a miss there reads as a clean no-match. The `.git` exclusion glob keeps the flag from reaching repository internals.\n    83\t- `--glob '!**/z_archive/**'`, `--glob '!**/node_modules/**'` and `--glob '!**/.git/**'` keep archived packets, vendored trees and repository internals out of the result set; the last one exists because `--hidden` would otherwise let ripgrep into `.git`.\n    84\t- `--include-archive` is the","truncated":true}
