{"type":"tool_result","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-96b13fce31ed126f","status":"completed","result":"=== create phase numbered docs ===\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/phase-definitions.md:33:Phases are suggested when a specification exceeds complexity thresholds that make single-folder management impractical. The phase decomposition workflow is offered only when both qualification thresholds in §2 are met. Child phases inside an already-established phase parent may use the documentation level appropriate to that child's work.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/phase-definitions.md:146:- **Lean trio:** A phase parent only requires **the lean trio** at the parent level: `spec.md`, `description.json`, `graph-metadata.json`.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/phase-definitions.md:171:**Detection rule (single source of truth):** A spec folder is a phase parent iff (a) it has at least one direct child matching `^[0-9]{3}-[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*$` AND (b) at least one such child has `spec.md` OR `description.json`. The shell helper `is_phase_parent()` (in `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/lib/shell-common.sh`) and the ESM JS helper `isPhaseParent()` (in `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/spec/is-phase-parent.js`) both implement this contract; they MUST agree on every input. The validator's phase-parent branches in `check-files.sh`, `check-level-match.sh`, `check-template-source.sh`, and the native `ANCHORS_VALID` rule in `runtime/lib/validation/orchestrator.ts` skip Level-N expectations when this returns true.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/phase-definitions.md:173:**Parent template:** New phase decompositions via `create.sh --phase --phases N --phase-names a,b,c` scaffold the parent from `templates/packet-types/phase-parent.spec.md.tmpl` and each child through the manifest-backed Level contract. The lean template carries an inline content-discipline comment listing FORBIDDEN narrative tokens (consolidation, merge, migration history) and REQUIRED content (root purpose, sub-phase control file, what needs done). This is a template convention rather than a validated rule.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/phase-definitions.md:175:**Tolerant migration policy:** Legacy phase parents that retain heavy docs (e.g. `026-graph-and-context-optimization/`) continue to validate without churn. Soft deprecation of legacy heavy docs is a separate follow-on packet.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/phase-definitions.md:199:| **Predecessor** | `../NNN-previous-phase/spec.md` |\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/phase-definitions.md:252:When the current work is already inside a phase parent:\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/phase-definitions.md:257:4. Use Option B only when the work is new or unrelated to the phase parent's purpose, requirements, and Phase Documentation Map.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/phase-definitions.md:260:The phase-qualification thresholds govern whether a standard packet may become a phase parent. An already-established phase parent does not need to requalify for every later child, but every new child must remain inside the parent's documented scope and use the documentation level appropriate to that child. A standard non-phased packet may not gain phase children unless both qualification thresholds are met, or the work qualifies for a series parent (§2), which takes the standard packet in as its first child.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/quick-reference.md:18:> Writing or amending a `goal.md`, including a phase parent's binding table? Use\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/quick-reference.md:265:**Phased Packets:** If the target already has child phase folders, use the active child when the work fits that child's scope. For a distinct but related workstream, prefer Option C and add the next sequential child phase instead of creating a new top-level sibling packet. Use the coordination root only for phase-map updates, root-scope decisions, and tree-truth verification. Use Option B only when the work is outside the phase parent's documented purpose and scope.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/quick-reference.md:279:- **Option C:** Use another existing packet — a related spec when it is a better scope match, a specific child under an existing phase parent, a related standard packet decomposed into phases when it meets both phase thresholds, a sub-track of the track (an existing one, or a new one proposed as a whole-track map when the track's packets have grown into clusters), or a series parent when the work is a different change to the same artifact as an existing packet in the same track (phase-definitions.md §2).\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/quick-reference.md:499:**Phase-Parent Resume Ladder**: When the resume target is a phase parent (folder has `[0-9]{3}-name/` children with `spec.md` or `description.json`), `/speckit:resume` honors the pointer first:\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/quick-reference.md:508:- A save aimed at a phase parent moves the pointers only when a `--full-auto` save routes its continuity into a leaf, and otherwise moves none.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/quick-reference.md:511:- No `_memory.continuity` block exists at a phase parent (per the lean trio policy: parents have no `implementation-summary.md`).\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/quick-reference.md:539:| `create.sh --phase --phases N --phase-names a,b,c <description>` | Create a phase parent and named child phase folders |\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/quick-reference.md:540:| `create.sh --phase --parent <parent> --phases N --phase-names a,b <description>` | Add named child phases to an existing parent spec |\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/feature-catalog/tooling-and-scripts/spec-lifecycle-automation.md:45:Archival is handled by `archive.sh`. A folder goes into the `z_archive/` beside it: `specs/z_archive/` for a packet at the specs root, the track's own `specs/<track>/z_archive/` for a packet in a track, a declared sub-track's own `z_archive/` for a packet in that sub-track and the parent's own `z_archive/` for a phase. A restore returns a folder to the home its archive sits in, under its own name when its number is free there. When a live packet under another name already holds that number, the restore does not reuse it: the packet takes the number after the highest one used in that home, live or archived, and `archive.sh` hands the move to `relocate.mjs --from <archived> --to <new> --apply` so every record that names the packet follows the new number. If `relocate.mjs` is missing or its run fails, the restore exits with status 1 and prints the command to rerun. A folder or link already at the exact restore path is refused. The list shows every archive's entries by the path that restores them. Archives are only looked for along packet homes: the specs root, tracks, declared sub-tracks and the numbered packets and phases reached from them. A folder without the sub-track declaration is not a home, so copies of a specs tree kept in a packet's research are never listed or restored from. After an archive and after a plain restore, the script re-derives the moved packet's recorded paths with `repair-derived.cjs`, asks the description cache's own producer to rebuild `specs/descriptions.json` when that file exists and runs `refresh-track-roots.mjs` for the track. A move inside a declared sub-track refreshes the sub-track's list and its track's list together, so each root's `children_ids` stays equal to its packets and the pre-push track-root gate has nothing to block. A tree without the cache file gets none, and a failed refresh is a warning that names the rerun and leaves the move in place. A phase parent's `children_ids` is left as it is: its writer adds children and drops one only through a reviewed prune, so an archived phase stays listed and a restore finds it there. Before archiving, it calls `calculate-completeness.sh --json` and enforces a default 90 percent minimum unless `--force`","truncated":true}
