{"type":"thinking","text":"The `search-presentation.txt` line 120 says path-only returns plain path strings with no such field, which is technically true for paths but omits `archivedPaths`. And line 128 says without flag \"record has no jsonLane\" (true) and \"rows carry no archived field\" (true for results/counts). It doesn't say no archivedPaths. But the field is always present. The docs should mention. Could be a traceability finding for path JSON contract.\n\n`search.md` line 116 says \"JSON rows of structured and count recipes carry archived...\" but not archivedPaths. This is in command docs (manifest). P1/P2.\n\n`README.txt` line 105 same. Great.\n\n`search-presentation.txt` line 120 is perhaps authoritative render contract; it explicitly says path-only returns plain path strings with no such field, which may lead a consumer to ignore or fail to render the new field. But the `archivedPaths` field isn't for display necessarily; it's a JSON path record. The goal says \"the --json path record marks the same paths\". So docs should mention it.\n\nThis is a strong iteration 3 finding.\n\nNeed check `search.md` line 116 perhaps says JSON rows only. So multiple docs.\n\nNow, what about `archivedPaths` added always, even without `--include-archive`. Is that intentional? Phase 009 D1 says \"The paths field of the path record keeps its type. The archived marker is an added field.\" It might be expected always; the test with includeArchive only. The docs saying \"Without the flag none of these fields appear\" is false for path recipe. Good.\n\nWe can produce a finding: \"Archive-search docs omit `archivedPaths` and claim the report shape is unchanged without the flag\" at `feature-catalog/retrieval/rg-archive-search.md:28`, also `retrieval-conventions.md:84`, `search.md:116`, `search-presentation.txt:120`, `README.md:1205`, `system-spec-kit/README.md:332`, `README.txt:105`. That's cross-consumer. Maybe P1 because docs contract for JSON output is wrong. The code is correct per phase goal; the docs are stale. Good.\n\nNeed maybe verify `search-presentation.txt` is in manifest? Yes. `search.md` yes. `README.txt` yes. Great.\n\nNow restore mixed-track docs. Could be iteration 2 security: A restore preflight refusal is a security-ish guard but docs omit. The user focus explicitly names mixed-track restore refusal. We need include. Finding primary `runtime/cli/spec/README.md:114`? Wait that file line 114 documents archive.sh and is mostly current but omits mixed-track. The `feature-catalog/tooling-and-scripts/spec-lifecycle-automation.md:45` is more detailed and omits. `manual-testing-playbook/tooling-and-scripts/spec-lifecycle-automation.md:219-220` says restore numbering but not mixed-track refusal. `changelog` line 145 says restore finds free number, not refusal. `system-spec-kit/README.md:638` says restore returns folder and next number but no mixed-track refusal. `README.md`? root doesn't have archive command details. So multiple docs.\n\nCould make P1: \"Restore docs omit the mixed-track refusal, so operators are told every numbered restore succeeds.\" Evidence: spec/README line 114 says `--restore` returns a folder; feature lifecycle line 45 says restore returns a folder; archive.sh lines 358-395 refuses; phase 008 AC. Impact: An operator with a track that has sub-tracks and an archived flat packet will see `Restore refused` with a migration command not documented; scripts may assume status 0. This is a correctness/traceability gap. P1 or P2? Because it's a documented command behavior with a new failure path, and the user focus calls it out. I'd say P1.\n\nCould be included in iteration 2 (security) because it prevents mixed tracks. But it's not a security vulnerability. Security dimension can be \"trust boundary checks around archive restore\" and we can report a documentation gap in the safety refusal. Maybe okay. Alternatively iteration 2 can be security-clean with no findings and note no security issues. But then we lose the mixed-track finding. We could put mixed-track in iteration 1 (correctness) or iteration 3 (traceability). Better iteration 2: security. The trust boundary is filesystem paths and archives; the docs omit a refusal that prevents mixed-track state. It's a security/maintainability issue? The dimension focus can be security, but finding class traceability. Dimensions can be multi. Hmm.\n\nMaybe iteration 2 security finding: \"Archive restore's mixed-track refusal is undocumented, so callers cannot know when a filesystem move is refused.\" That's a security boundary change. Fine.\n\nAnother security finding: docs for `--include-archive` imply it searches only `specs/`? It searches `.skilled` too, but not relevant. Paths in JSON include archived, no injection. No real security bug. Could note confirmed-clean surfaces: symlink/path traversal refusals documented and code confirms; secrets none; no untrusted input executed. That's acceptable.\n\nNeed perhaps search docs and code for path handling to verify no security issue. `archive.sh` has symlink refusal, `relocate` confine, `rg-wrapper` no shell. We can say security dimension found only a documentation gap about mixed-track refusal. Good.\n\nIteration 3 traceability: `archivedPaths` docs + `suggest-subtracks` non-NNN refusal + phase 007 rewrite docs + feature catalog test inventory. That's 3 P1/P2. We need not overload. Maybe:\nR3-P1-006: Archived path JSON field missing from all docs.\nR3-P1-007: `folder-structure.md:141` and `sub-track-proposal.md:30` omit the non-NNN-slug refusal.\nR3-P2-008: `spec-packet-relocation.md:44` omits bare-name and pre-archive rewrite, and line 91 test table omits new suites. That's a P2 documentation completeness.\nCould be 2 P1 + 1 P2.\n\nIteration 4 maintainability: Changelog/test count drift and duplicate description divergence. Perhaps:\nR4-P2-009: Changelog `v2.13.0.0.md` test counts stale (2567 vs 2603) and no entry for phases 006-010; release notes no longer describe the code at version 2.13.0.0. This is maintainability/traceability. P2 maybe.\nR4-P2-010: Command descriptions duplicated across root README, skill README, spec README, feature catalog with inconsistent detail, so the same behavior is documented four ways and already diverges (e.g., exit 3 only in spec README). Recommend single-source link. P2.\nR4-P2-011: Feature catalog root line 690 duplicates leaf and omits exit 3; manual playbook scenario 492 only covers 001-005 behavior and doesn't exercise 006-010. P2.\nIteration 4 might have 3 P2 (weighted 3) with new ratio maybe 1.0. That delays convergence. Fine.\n\nIteration 5 final cross-check maybe no new findings, newFindingsRatio 0.0; stop. But the loop must cover maintainability in iteration 4, then iteration 5 could be \"final verification / cross-reference consistency\" with no findings. Convergence threshold 0.1 with ratio 0.0; should stop. But after iteration 1-4, all dimensions covered, active P1s exist. Convergence can still happen despite active P1? The loop may stop with CONDITIONAL and remediation. Likely. But the workflow's convergence check has gates: if active P0/P1? It may continue until all dimensions covered and no new findings for two iterations. Let's inspect convergence logic."}
