{"type":"tool_result","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-804eed2268d0d17d","status":"completed","result":"    40\tFrozen choices. Changing one is an amendment.\n    41\t\n    42\t| ID | Decision |\n    43\t|----|----------|\n    44\t| D1 | `relocate.mjs` gains one exit status, 3, for a run whose steps and sweep all passed and whose only failure is a moved packet's validation. Statuses 0, 1 and 2 keep their meaning otherwise. A run that ends on 3 has landed, so the same call again prints `relocate: nothing to do` and exits 0. This amends decision D1 of phase 006 for that one case, and REQ-001 and REQ-012 of phase 001, as the parent `spec.md` Operator Decisions record. |\n    45\t| D2 | A renumbering restore that ends on status 3 exits 0 and names the packet in a warning. |\n    46\t| D3 | `archive.sh` holds no copy of the sub-track predicate. It asks the relocate preflight and refuses a restore only on its mixed-track line. Every other preflight result leaves the restore as it is today. |\n    47\t| D4 | A new phase is numbered above every live and archived phase of its parent, and its predecessor is the highest live phase. |\n    48\t| D5 | Every finding is reproduced in a `mktemp` copy before its fix, and no code comment names a spec path, a packet number or a finding id. |\n    49\t\n    50\t<!-- /ANCHOR:directive -->\n=== feature lifecycle line 45 ===\n    43\t### Post-Action Behavior\n    44\t\n    45\tArchival is handled by `archive.sh`. A folder goes into the `z_archive/` beside it: `specs/z_archive/` for a packet at the specs root, the track's own `specs/<track>/z_archive/` for a packet in a track, a declared sub-track's own `z_archive/` for a packet in that sub-track and the parent's own `z_archive/` for a phase. A restore returns a folder to the home its archive sits in, under its own name when its number is free there. When a live packet under another name already holds that number, the restore does not reuse it: the packet takes the number after the highest one used in that home, live or archived, and `archive.sh` hands the move to `relocate.mjs --from <archived> --to <new> --apply` so every record that names the packet follows the new number. If `relocate.mjs` is missing or its run fails, the restore exits with status 1 and prints the command to rerun. A folder or link already at the exact restore path is refused. The list shows every archive's entries by the path that restores them. Archives are only looked for along packet homes: the specs root, tracks, declared sub-tracks and the numbered packets and phases reached from them. A folder without the sub-track declaration is not a home, so copies of a specs tree kept in a packet's research are never listed or restored from. After an archive and after a plain restore, the script re-derives the moved packet's recorded paths with `repair-derived.cjs`, asks the description cache's own producer to rebuild `specs/descriptions.json` when that file exists and runs `refresh-track-roots.mjs` for the track. A move inside a declared sub-track refreshes the sub-track's list and its track's list together, so each root's `children_ids` stays equal to its packets and the pre-push track-root gate has nothing to block. A tree without the cache file gets none, and a failed refresh is a warning that names the rerun and leaves the move in place. A phase parent's `children_ids` is left as it is: its writer adds children and drops one only through a reviewed prune, so an archived phase stays listed and a restore finds it there. Before archiving, it calls `calculate-completeness.sh --json` and enforces a default 90 percent minimum unless `--force` is used or the operator confirms archiving a less-complete spec. The script also validates `NNN-name` folder naming, refuses to archive outside the specs root, detects folders already under any `z_archive/`, and uses a copy-to-temp-then-rename flow before deleting the source folder. A `z_archive/` that is a symlink is refused on archive and on restore, and `--list` leaves a linked archive out without naming it. A linked packet folder is archived by its real path when that path stays inside the specs root. An archive or restore path with a `.` or `..` segment is refused before anything resolves it, since such a segment can reach a linked `z_archive/` that the literal path does not show.\n    46\t\n=== spec README archive ===\n   114\t| `archive.sh` | Moves completed or stale spec folders into the `z_archive/` beside them: `specs/z_archive/` for a packet at the specs root, `specs/<track>/z_archive/` for a packet in a track, the sub-track's own `z_archive/` for a packet in a sub-track and the parent's own `z_archive/` for a phase. `--restore` returns a folder to the place its archive belongs to. When a live packet with another name already holds its number, the restore lands on the next free number above every live and archived packet there, through `relocate.mjs --from <archived> --to <next> --apply`. `--list` shows every archive's entries by the path that restores them. Archives are found beside the specs root, each track, each declared sub-track and the numbered folders beneath them, without following links, so a copy of a specs tree kept in a packet's research is left out. After every move it runs `repair-derived.cjs` over the moved folder, so the packet and each packet inside it record their new path, and in a track it runs `refresh-track-roots.mjs` for that track, so the track root's `children_ids` follows the packet. After an archive or a plain restore it also asks `ensureDescriptionCache` to rebuild the description cache `specs/descriptions.json` when that file exists, so the cache drops the row for the folder that is gone. A failed rebuild warns and leaves the move in place. A phase parent's `graph-metadata.json` is left as it is, because its writer drops a child only through a reviewed prune. A `z_archive/` that is a symlink is refused on archive and on restore, and `--list` leaves a linked archive out without naming it. An archive or restore path with a `.` or `..` segment is refused before anything resolves it. |\n=== skill README archive ===\n   638\t| `archive.sh` | archive completed spec folders into the `z_archive/` beside them, which for a packet in a declared sub-track is the sub-track's own `z_archive/`. `--restore` returns a folder to the place its archive belongs to, and when a live packet already holds its number it lands on the next free number, counting archived packets, through `relocate.mjs`. After an archive or a plain restore the description cache `specs/descriptions.json` is rebuilt when it exists |\n=== changelog restore ===\n   143\t#### A Restore Finds a Free Number\n   144\t\n   145\t`archive.sh --restore` returns a packet to the folder its archive belongs to. A live packet with another name may now hold its number. The restore then lands on the next free number through `relocate.mjs`. In one check, `001-old` came back as `011-old` beside a live `001-holder`. A restore onto a live folder with the same name is still refused.\n   146\t\n"}
